Chapter: UnitPatternLit
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A literal pattern must match on a unit type exactly as it does on the
 type inside the wrapper. `emit-pattern-lit-test` dispatches on the
 scrutinee type, and until 2026-07-28 it dispatched on the WRAPPER: a
 unit Text missed the TextTy arm and fell to the otherwise arm, which
 compares the register against `pat-lit-to-integer` of the spelling.
 The register holds a pointer, so the arm could never match:
 `when (Name "sin") is "sin"` took the catchall. Measured on seed
 759D3E0F and again, identically, on 6ED0A257.

 This one is silent, which is why it is worth a test of its own rather
 than a line in the show pin. Nothing is printed oddly and nothing
 faults; the program simply takes a branch it should not have taken.
 The same hole in `show` at least produced a visibly absurd number.

 Two controls, and the test means nothing without them. `text-plain`
 is the same match on an unwrapped Text and must match under every
 reading -- without it, a compiler that had broken literal patterns
 outright would look identical here. `int-unit` is a literal pattern on
 a unit Integer, where the otherwise arm is the CORRECT destination, so
 it must match under every reading too; it is the row that says the fix
 strips the wrapper rather than sending everything to the Text arm.

Section: Types

  Name = unit Text
  Count = unit Integer

Section: Match

  classify-plain : Text -> Text
  classify-plain (s) =
   when s
    is "sin" -> "matched"
    is otherwise -> "fell-through"

  classify-unit : Name -> Text
  classify-unit (n) =
   when n
    is "sin" -> "matched"
    is otherwise -> "fell-through"

  classify-int : Count -> Text
  classify-int (c) =
   when c
    is 42 -> "matched"
    is otherwise -> "fell-through"

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("text-plain=" & classify-plain "sin")
    print-line-uni ("text-unit =" & classify-unit (Name "sin"))
    print-line-uni ("int-unit  =" & classify-int (Count 42))
  end
